<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasion of the Khwarazmian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1219">1219</a>–<a href="/years/1221">1221</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as full-scale invasion by Genghis Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> was the campaign of 1219 to 1221 launched by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> Shah's governor killed Mongol envoys and merchants. Mongol armies captured and devastated the great cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. The Khwarazmian Empire collapsed, and its last ruler, Jalal al-Din, fought on in exile.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Khwarazmian dynasty had expanded from its homeland to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> in the late 12th and early 13th centuries. Tekish declared himself a sovereign in 1189 and was accepted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> in 1198, and his son Muhammad II, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshah</a> in 1200, subjugated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Qarakhanids</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. Genghis Khan had meanwhile united the Mongolic peoples. Relations were at first cordial but soured through a series of diplomatic provocations.</p><p>The size of the armies is much disputed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a> gives 100,000 to 135,000 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, while the later chroniclers Rashid al-Din and Juzjani give far higher figures that modern historians regard as inflated. Muhammad distrusted most of his commanders and chose to divide his troops among his main cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Mongol princes Chagatai and Ögedei besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otrar">Otrar</a>, which held out for five months before a traitor opened its gate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and his youngest son Tolui took about 50,000 men across the Kyzyl Kum desert and appeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, which fell after a break-out force was destroyed; a fire razed most of the city, and artisans were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Genghis reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> in March 1220. He exploited the quarrel between the shah and his mother Terken Khatun, and the city’s garrison surrendered on the fifth day. The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurganj">Gurganj</a> proved the hardest of the campaign.</p><p>In 1221 Tolui devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> the townspeople surrendered on promises of fair treatment, but the population was driven onto the plain and put to the sword, apart from a few artisans and children. Ibn al-Athir put the dead at 700,000 and Juvayni at 1,300,000. Muhammad II fled before the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and died destitute on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. His son Jalal al-Din defeated a Mongol general at Parwan but was crushed by Genghis at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Genghis returned to the war against the Jin dynasty in 1223. Jalal al-Din tried to rebuild the Khwarazmian kingdom and retook western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, but he was defeated by the Rum Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> at Yassıçemen in 1230 and died near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakir</a> in 1231. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under Chormaghun came back in 1230 and 1231 to conquer the west of the former empire. The campaign was the first in which the Mongols defeated a non-sinicised state, and it gave them a base for later attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and for the invasion of Rus’. The Persian lands later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the northern cities to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol invasion of the Khwarazmian Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1218 the massacre of Mongol-sponsored merchants at Otrar and Ala ad-Din Muhammad II's execution of a Mongol envoy provoked a <b>full-scale invasion by Genghis Khan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
